27
}}}
\par 
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \i0 \fs20 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Secondly, Art.1 expressly acknowledges the possibility of modification or exclusion of UCP provisions. A credit may, for example, provide for signature of a document that the UCP states need not be signed, or may shorten or lengthen a UCP time period. 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Effect will be given to such terms, the UCP being applied as amended by the credit. Likewise, some 
}
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat42 
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
credits
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat2 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 deny any liability of the issuing bank for the correspondent bank\u8217\'3fs charges,
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs16 
{\*\bkmkstart co_fnRef_6f4efbe4-0d96-43be-86d0-f7e1ebb}{\*\bkmkend co_fnRef_6f4efbe4-0d96-43be-86d0-f7e1ebb}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#co_footnote_6f4efbe4-0d96-43be-86d0-f7e1" }{\fldrslt 
{\super \b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs15 \sa0 \sb0 
28
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb200 
 but a correspondent bank is not, of course, obliged to accept the request to act under such a credit.
\par 
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \i0 \fs20 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Thirdly, the more that a suggested interpretation of an express term of the credit departs from the commercial essence of a 
}
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat42 
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
documentary
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat2 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 credit, the less likely it is that such an interpretation will reflect the intentions of the parties and the less likely it is, therefore, that a court will accept it. In 
}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.westlaw.com/Link/Document/FullText?findType=Y&serNum=1986026480&pubNum=4891&originatingDoc=I8CC6FA006E3211E782BDB54604FBEE92&refType=UC&originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=CommentaryUKLink&contextData=(sc.Search)" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Forestal Mimosa
}
}}}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
,
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs16 
{\*\bkmkstart co_fnRef_66df5a08-0303-4113-aa04-0290a28}{\*\bkmkend co_fnRef_66df5a08-0303-4113-aa04-0290a28}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#co_footnote_66df5a08-0303-4113-aa04-0290" }{\fldrslt 
{\super \b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs15 \sa0 \sb0 
29
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 the credit was available by \u8220\'3fby acceptance of your drafts \u8230\'3f drawn on [the applicant]\u8221\'3f and further stated that \u8220\'3fdrafts accepted within the terms of this credit will be duly honoured at maturity\u8221\'3f. The defendant confirming bank argued that, by virtue of this wording, the credit became operative only if the applicant duly accepted a draft. The applicant having refused to accept the draft, the confirming bank argued that the beneficiary had no rights under the credit. Sir John Megaw pointed out that such an interpretation was incompatible with the commercial understanding of a 
}
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat42 
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
documentary
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat2 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 credit. It was of the essence of such an instrument that it should not be open to the applicant to \u8220\'3frender the bank\u8217\'3fs obligation to the [beneficiary] under the credit a wholly useless obligation and one that has no legal effect\u8221\'3f.
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs16 
{\*\bkmkstart co_fnRef_18a2da88-1e81-46f4-9493-b019665}{\*\bkmkend co_fnRef_18a2da88-1e81-46f4-9493-b019665}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#co_footnote_18a2da88-1e81-46f4-9493-b019" }{\fldrslt 
{\super \b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs15 \sa0 \sb0 
30
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 The wording of the credit was duly held to reflect the wording of the applicable 
}
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat42 
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
revision
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat2 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 of the UCP
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs16 
{\*\bkmkstart co_fnRef_7b63eb1d-30b3-46e3-8ad3-e0e3a3f}{\*\bkmkend co_fnRef_7b63eb1d-30b3-46e3-8ad3-e0e3a3f}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#co_footnote_7b63eb1d-30b3-46e3-8ad3-e0e3" }{\fldrslt 
{\super \b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs15 \sa0 \sb0 
31
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 and not to undermine the responsibility of the confirming bank under that 
}
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat42 
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
revision
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat2 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb200 
 for both the acceptance of a draft drawn on the applicant and for its payment on maturity.
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs16 
{\*\bkmkstart co_fnRef_5f8a1fb9-0e5c-4607-b417-96a3fd1}{\*\bkmkend co_fnRef_5f8a1fb9-0e5c-4607-b417-96a3fd1}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#co_footnote_5f8a1fb9-0e5c-4607-b417-96a3" }{\fldrslt 
{\super \b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs15 \sa0 \sb0 
32
}}}
\par 
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \i0 \fs20 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
In similar vein, the more bespoke terms depart from core propositions of the UCP, the clearer the notice of the terms required for their incorporation into the credit. For example, the UCP contemplates that, where presented documents are non-compliant, the presenter retains control over the documents unless the issuing bank accepts liability to pay. In 
}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.westlaw.com/Link/Document/FullText?findType=Y&serNum=2059449646&pubNum=6821&originatingDoc=I8CC6FA006E3211E782BDB54604FBEE92&refType=UC&originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=CommentaryUKLink&contextData=(sc.Search)" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Heytex Bramsche GmbH v Unity Trade Capital Ltd
}
}}}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
,
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs16 
{\*\bkmkstart co_fnRef_7f9e4424-6fb8-496a-a2c5-e18d411}{\*\bkmkend co_fnRef_7f9e4424-6fb8-496a-a2c5-e18d411}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#co_footnote_7f9e4424-6fb8-496a-a2c5-e18d" }{\fldrslt 
{\super \b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs15 \sa0 \sb0 
33
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb200 
 it was argued that the credit incorporated terms that permitted the credit issuer to release the documents to the applicant without incurring liability for payment to the beneficiary, leaving the beneficiary without goods, documents or money, and able to look for payment only to the applicant. The requisite clear and explicit notice was not present. Among other failings, there was no express reference to the relevant UCP provisions. The credit, moreover, was issued by SWIFT message and the only reference to the source of the allegedly incorporated terms was in the wrong field for incorporating terms affecting the position of the beneficiary.
\par 
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \i0 \fs20 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Fourthly, no matter how contrary to accepted principle, a bespoke term once interpreted must generally be applied. A credit that required presentation of a document issued or signed by the applicant would be open to the same criticism as the suggested interpretation of the credit in 
}
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.westlaw.com/Link/Document/FullText?findType=Y&serNum=1986026480&pubNum=4891&originatingDoc=I8CC6FA006E3211E782BDB54604FBEE92&refType=UC&originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=CommentaryUKLink&contextData=(sc.Search)" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Forestal Mimosa
}
}}}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
, and would clearly be extremely ill-advised from the beneficiary\u8217\'3fs point of view, but the banks would have no discretion to dispense with the need for the offending document. Thus, current 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs20 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
ICC
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 rules provide that 
}
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat42 
{\b1 \cf24 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
credits
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs20 \chcbpat2 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 should not require such a document, but if it is required the beneficiary must either seek an amendment of the credit or comply with the requirement and bear the risk of failure to do so.
}
